{
  "example": "A definition named `glossary.first_parent_history` declares the phrase \"first-parent history\". A term reference to that name renders the phrase as an ordinary hyperlink to the declaration, without displaying the identifier or a badge. {cite}`requirements.definitions.references`",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the example is covered by one test, `phrases_are_plain_links_and_markdown_literals_are_preserved` in `tests/definitions.rs` (lines 204–239). It carries a `{test}` reference to `requirements.definitions.phrase_rendering`. One expected outcome is only implied by the assertions, not asserted.\n\n**Scenario.** A definition declares a phrase, and a `{term}` reference renders that phrase as an ordinary hyperlink to the declaration. There is no identifier display and no badge.\n\n**What the test sets up and asserts**\n- It builds a book with a `glossary.md` chapter declaring `:::{definition} a` with `:phrase: *alpha* & <beta>`. A second chapter, `guide/deep.md`, references it with `{term}`glossary.a``.\n- The declaration is anchored: the glossary chapter contains `id=\"habeas-definition-glossary.a\"`.\n- The `:phrase:` option line is not leaked into the rendered declaration.\n- There is no badge: the referencing chapter has no `shields.io`.\n- The reference is a hyperlink to the declaration: exactly one `href=\"../glossary.html#habeas-definition-glossary.a\"` appears. The relative path from `guide/deep.md` is correct.\n- The phrase is displayed and escaped: `&#42;alpha&#42; &amp; &lt;beta&gt;`.\n- The same fixture holds a code-span reference, a fenced-code reference and a backslash-escaped reference. The assertion of exactly one link shows these stay literal.\n\n**Implementation, checked for context.** In `src/lib.rs`, `replace_references` handles `ReferenceKind::Term` separately. For a term target it emits only `<a href=\"…\">{escaped_phrase}</a>`, with no badge image, no class and no identifier text. `terms::escaped_phrase` in `src/terms.rs` entity-escapes Markdown and HTML metacharacters. `replace_definitions` emits the anchor for the declaration. This matches the example.\n\n**Minor limitations.** The test uses `glossary.a` and the phrase `*alpha* & <beta>` instead of the example's literal `glossary.first_parent_history` and \"first-parent history\". It never asserts that the identifier is absent from the visible link text. That outcome follows from the code and from the assertion that the phrase is the link content, but it is not checked directly. Neither limitation is serious enough to fail the example.",
  "inputs": [
